  the same caller-supplied meets/misses policy table as count telemetry;
  insufficient sample evidence defers, and exact latency evidence plus
  violations remain attached.

  A parallel request-scoped publication boundary consumes latency
  recommendations. Deferral preserves the request unchanged, while ready
  evidence replaces only its retry policy and retains previous/current policy
  plus exact latency recommendation evidence.

  Retry policy now owns an immutable canonical snapshot containing exact maximum
  native attempts and either complete fallback or one positive sliced fallback
  budget. Reconstruction uses that representation directly instead of scheduler
  behavior, and the internal sliced form is nonzero by construction. Two cases
  cover complete and sliced round trips. This remains transfer evidence only;
  durable/global policy ownership is still pending.

  A fixed 32-byte revision-one policy codec now preserves that snapshot as
  canonical little-endian transfer bytes. Decode validates framing, reserved
  fields, explicit fallback tag, complete/sliced budget semantics, host integer
  representation, and exact length before reconstruction. Five cases cover both
  canonical forms plus semantic, framing, and length rejection. Storage and
  cross-cycle publication remain separate pending authority.

  Typed retry-policy persistence now binds that codec to the generalized opaque
  blob service. Missing state stays explicit, corrupt bytes fail codec
  validation, byte limits reject before outbound publication, and durability
  confirmation preserves confirmed or committed-failure evidence. Five
  adapter-neutral cases cover those paths and one host-real filesystem case
  round-trips a sliced policy durably.

  This remains one caller-selected policy document only. Active ownership and
  typed conditional publication are separate boundaries described below; future
  requests are still never mutated automatically.

  A process-local active-policy owner now carries one exact retry policy and a
  monotonic `u64` revision across cached cycles. Revision zero is initial; a
  matching expected revision publishes once, stale revisions conflict without
  mutation, and revision exhaustion fails closed. Four cases cover initial,
  published, conflict, and exhausted states. Validated state can be
  reconstructed explicitly; durable conditional revision publication is
  described below.

  An explicit request-binding boundary now consumes one immutable active state
  plus one future cached-cycle request. It replaces only that request policy
  and retains active revision/state plus the previous request policy. One case
  covers revisioned binding. Requests do not subscribe to the owner and future
  requests
  are never mutated implicitly.

  Revisioned active state now has a fixed 52-byte revision-one codec carrying
  the exact `u64` owner revision plus the existing canonical 32-byte policy
  frame. Outer and nested framing both fail closed; two cases cover exact round
  trip and corruption. Typed persistence durably restores that state and can
  reconstruct the owner.

  One adapter-neutral and one host-real filesystem case cover durable round
  trips.

  The opaque blob port/application boundary now also supports bounded
  compare-and-swap publication. Exact mismatch bytes return as conflict
  evidence; successful publication can then confirm durability without
  reclassifying a committed sync failure as rollback. The filesystem adapter
  uses one persistent sibling advisory lock across compare plus staged rename,
  and ordinary replacement honors that same lock.

  Two adapter-neutral cases cover match and conflict, one covers committed
  durability failure, and one host-real race proves exactly one cooperating
  missing-state publisher commits. Arbitrary writers that bypass the adapter
  remain outside this lock contract.

  Typed active-policy CAS composes that conditional blob path with the
  revisioned state codec and existing owner transition. Missing state
  initializes zero; present expected state advances exactly one revision, while
  stale state
  returns the decoded current owner without mutation. Four adapter-neutral cases
  cover initialization, update, conflict, and exhaustion; one host-real race
  proves exactly one initializer commits.

  Count and latency recommendations now publish ready policy through that
  durable active-state CAS boundary. One shared typed result keeps the exact
  recommendation beside durable, committed-sync-failure, or conflict evidence.
  Deferred recommendations perform zero storage work.

  Three count cases cover deferral, revision-zero initialization, and stale
  conflict; one latency case advances an existing active revision. Request
  binding stays explicit.

  Exact histogram coarsening removes only caller-selected interior source
  bounds while preserving bucket totals, extrema, overall samples, exact
  nanosecond totals, and overflow-bin evidence. Targets must be ordered source
  subsets with the same final bound; unsupported target drift fails closed.
  Five cases cover identity, exact coarsening, missing-bound rejection,
  overflow-bound rejection, and normalization before same-schema merge.

  Exact refinement requires an additional caller-supplied sample witness. The
  finer histogram is rebuilt from those samples, then coarsened to the source
  schema and required to equal every source count, total, extremum, and overflow
  value. Persisted histogram state alone still cannot invent a finer
  distribution. Three cases cover valid refinement, incomplete witness, and a
  target schema that omits a source boundary.

  A pure common-schema derivation intersects two ordered schemas when their
  final overflow boundary matches. It
  returns the greatest exact shared bounds plus per-side removal counts; four
  cases cover identity, partial-overlap merge, final-bound-only reduction, and
  incompatible final bounds.

  One normalized exact merge now derives that common schema, coarsens both
  inputs independently, and invokes the existing transactional same-schema
  merge. It retains per-side removed-bound counts and leaves both sources
  unchanged. Two cases cover compatible different schemas and incompatible
  final bounds.

  One-shot durable latency merge now loads bounded raw bytes, decodes exact
  current state, normalizes/merges once, and conditionally publishes against
  those exact loaded bytes. Missing state initializes from the submitted source.
  Conflict returns decoded current/expected state without hidden retry, while
  post-CAS durability failure remains committed publication evidence.

  Five deterministic cases cover initialization, normalization, conflict, sync
  failure, and incompatible schema; one host-real filesystem case crosses the
  complete durable merge path.

  A synchronous retry layer accepts a positive caller-selected maximum attempt
  count. Only typed CAS conflicts retry, and each retry starts a fresh bounded
  load/merge/CAS attempt. Durable commits, committed sync failures, and
  prepublication errors stop immediately.

  Four cases cover first-attempt success, conflict refresh, final conflict
  retention, and committed failure. No sleep or backoff policy is inferred.


  Latency merge retry now also accepts the shared synchronous retry directive.
  A small immutable request carries source telemetry plus byte/attempt bounds.
  After a retryable conflict, the caller sees the exact completed-attempt count
  and may return `Continue` or `Stop`; stopping preserves that typed conflict.
  Continued attempts still perform a fresh bounded load/merge/CAS, while the
  runtime performs no clock read, wait, backoff, or fairness action.

  Two focused cases cover continue and stop directives.

  Cached-cycle composition binds the canonical count/latency codecs to an
  explicit persistence application service and reconstructs exact validated
  owners after load. The application treats payloads as opaque bytes and checks
  a positive bound before publication and again after adapter loads.

  The storage-neutral outbound blob port and bounded application service now
  name only opaque single-blob transport, byte limits, and publication state.
  They expose no paths, filesystem APIs, policy selection, or payload
  interpretation; typed telemetry codecs remain composition clients. Six
  adapter-neutral cases retain both telemetry round trips, missing state, byte
  guards, and store failures across that generalized boundary.

  A separate outbound capability now requires one preconfigured opaque blob pair
  to load and replace atomically. Partial pair state is unrepresentable; failed
  replacement preserves the complete prior pair, and sequential single-blob
  writes do not satisfy the contract. The application service enforces separate
  positive bounds before publication and after load, while post-commit pair
  durability remains distinct from rollback.

  Six deterministic cases cover atomic replacement, preserved prior state, both
  write bounds, oversized adapter output, missing state, and committed
  durability
  failure.

  Cached-cycle composition now fixes canonical count-window bytes as the first
  pair member and canonical latency-histogram bytes as the second. Atomic
  restore
  loads both before independently repeating their existing codec and snapshot
  validation. Missing pair state invents neither side, and committed durability
  failure retains the complete new pair.

  Five deterministic cases cover typed atomic round trip, missing state, count
  corruption, latency corruption, and committed durability failure.

  A concrete filesystem pair adapter now binds one explicit manifest path. It
  writes and synchronizes two immutable same-directory generation members, then
  writes and synchronizes a staging manifest; atomic manifest `rename` is the
  sole commit point. Readers follow one complete manifest to immutable members
  and cannot combine generations.

  A persistent sibling lock now coordinates cooperating readers and publishers.
  Readers hold a shared lock from manifest observation through both immutable
  member reads; generation creation, conditional comparison, and manifest
  replacement hold the exclusive lock. This removes the manifest-to-member race
  that would otherwise make later generation reclamation unsafe.

  Crashes before manifest commit may still leave ignored orphan generations,
  while directory sync remains explicit post-commit durability confirmation.

  Explicit generation reclamation now runs under the same exclusive sibling
  lock. It validates the current manifest and verifies both current members
  exist before deletion, and accepts only exact generation names reconstructed
  through the adapter naming rule. Missing manifest state treats all exact owned
  members as unreferenced; non-UTF-8 manifest basenames fail before deletion.

  Reclamation attempts every eligible deletion and retains completed removals
  beside exact failed paths and host error kinds. Directory sync after deletion
  is committed durability evidence, not rollback. Repeated passes rescan
  idempotently, and publication itself still performs no automatic cleanup.

  Callers may additionally preserve exact opaque revisions they already own.
  The adapter compares revisions for equality only and does not infer chronology
  or choose a retention window.


  An optional storage-neutral pair-reclamation port now carries opaque revision,
  reclamation-evidence, and pre-cleanup-error ownership without filesystem
  types. A dedicated application use case forwards one caller-selected exact
  preserve slice unchanged and delegates one pass. One adapter-neutral case
  proves exact forwarding; the host-real preservation case crosses application,
  port, and filesystem adapter together.


  A process-local exact-retention owner now records only opaque revisions the
  caller explicitly retains or releases. Duplicate retain is a no-op, release
  uses exact equality, and insertion order has no temporal semantics. The
  reclamation request borrows that preserve slice directly; one focused case
  covers membership and the forwarding case consumes it.

  Six focused cases cover current-generation preservation and foreign-name
  filtering, caller-preserved revisions, current-member prevalidation,
  missing-manifest idempotency, partial deletion evidence, and
  malformed-manifest
  rejection before any eligible orphan is removed.

  Pair CAS now compares an opaque publication revision rather than pair payload
  bytes. Versioned load returns the bounded pair plus revision observed from the
  same manifest commit point, and `None` matches only missing state. Application
  orchestration rechecks both member bounds and confirms durability only after a
  committed CAS.

  The filesystem adapter uses an epoch/generation manifest token as the opaque
  revision. Initial publication seeds the epoch once; later cooperating writers
  preserve it and derive the next non-wrapping generation from the current
  manifest under the persistent sibling lock. Crash-orphan collisions are
  skipped inside the same epoch, and stale revisions conflict even when newer
  payloads are byte-identical.

  Five adapter-neutral cases cover versioned load, missing initialization, stale
  conflict, post-load bounds, and committed durability failure. Two host-real
  CAS
  cases cover revision advance and byte-identical ABA rejection.


  Three host-real generation-identity cases cover orphan collision skipping in
  one epoch, generation continuation through reclamation with a fresh adapter,
  and fail-closed `u64` generation exhaustion. The 24-byte manifest encoding is
  unchanged; its first `u64` is now the persisted store epoch rather than the
  identity of every publishing process.


  The opaque filesystem revision now also has a separate canonical 24-byte
  `MBPREV01` codec carrying magic plus little-endian epoch and generation.
  Decode rejects wrong size, magic, and zero generation while retaining opaque
  semantics. Three focused cases cover canonical round trip and malformed
  representations.


  Exact retained-revision sets now also have canonical `MBPRET01` framing:
  magic, a little-endian `u64` count, and canonical 24-byte revisions in caller
  order. Encode and decode reject duplicates; decode also rejects malformed
  nested revisions and count/length drift. Four focused cases cover exact round
  trip plus duplicate, framing, and nested-member rejection without assigning
  chronology or retention priority to the preserved order.


  A typed composition journal now persists those exact retention frames through
  the existing bounded durable blob service. Missing storage remains explicit,
  present bytes rebuild the exact process-local retention owner, and
  post-publication durability failure remains committed evidence. Three focused
  cases cover missing state, durable round trip, and committed durability
  failure without adding retention selection or cleanup scheduling.


  The typed journal also supports one-shot conditional durable publication over
  the generic blob CAS. Expected retention is encoded canonically; conflict
  returns exact current bounded bytes decoded into typed retention, malformed
  conflict state fails closed, and only successful publication confirms
  durability. Four focused cases cover missing-state initialization, typed
  conflict, corrupt conflict rejection, and committed durability failure.

  One host-real cross-instance case advances the journal through one file store,
  then proves a stale second store receives typed conflict evidence for the
  advanced retention and cannot overwrite it. This operation never retries,
  merges, reorders, or selects retained revisions. Automatic conflict retry
  remains pending because refreshing expected state and replaying a whole-set
  replacement would discard concurrent caller-selected retention unless an
  explicit reconciliation policy chooses the new set.


  A separate caller-directed reconciliation layer now supplies safe bounded
  retry without choosing that policy. It performs one typed restore, invokes a
  caller callback before every CAS attempt, and feeds each typed conflict into
  the callback before the next attempt. Five focused cases cover missing
  initialization, refreshed conflict reconciliation, exhausted-budget conflict,
  callback rejection before CAS, and committed durability failure stopping
  after one attempt. Union/intersection, revision ordering, backoff, and
  automatic retention policy remain caller-owned or pending.


  Retention-journal retry now also accepts the shared synchronous retry
  directive. A small request carries the positive byte and attempt bounds.
  After a retryable conflict, the caller sees the exact completed-attempt count
  and may return `Continue` or `Stop`; stopping preserves that typed conflict.
  Continued retries feed the exact current journal into caller reconciliation
  again, while the runtime performs no clock read, wait, backoff, or fairness
  action.

  Two focused cases cover continue and stop directives.


  Durable retention state and generation reclamation still lack one shared
  serialization boundary. The generic file-blob journal and pair-generation
  adapter use different sibling locks, so load-journal-then-reclaim can race a
  concurrent journal writer and delete a revision that became retained after
  the load. Do not add that orchestration until journal mutation and generation
  deletion share a lock or transaction boundary. Process-local callers that own
  all retention mutation may coordinate the two operations externally.


  The reviewed next prerequisite is a reusable filesystem coordination object
  that owns one explicit lock path and yields non-cloneable shared/exclusive
  guard tokens. Journal and pair adapters may bind to the same coordinator, and
  internal prelocked operations must require the matching guard so one
  cross-object transaction acquires the lock only once. This mirrors the
  compiler progress-sidecar writer transaction. Reusing one lock filename with
  today's independently locking public methods is insufficient because nested
  acquisition can deadlock and cannot prove that every mutation joined the same
  serialization boundary.


  The reusable coordinator is now implemented. Existing blob and pair
  constructors preserve their legacy sibling locks, while explicit
  `with_coordination(...)` constructors bind either adapter to one caller-owned
  lock domain. Guards are non-cloneable and retain exact lock-path identity.

  Two host-real cases cover shared-guard identity and unchanged blob/pair
  storage behavior under one coordinator.

  Guarded prelocked operations are now implemented for bounded blob load/CAS and
  pair-generation reclamation. Each requires one matching exclusive guard and
  rejects foreign guard identity before state access or deletion. One host-real
  case spans blob CAS/load and pair reclamation under one acquisition; another
  proves both adapters reject a foreign guard.

  Journal-driven reclamation is now implemented under that shared guard. Missing
  journal state performs no deletion, while a present canonical journal supplies
  the exact preservation set for one reclamation pass. Two host-real cases cover
  missing-journal safety and exact durable preservation.

  A separate one-shot transition now CASes the canonical retention journal,
  confirms journal durability, and only then reclaims using the replacement
  retention under the same guard. Conflict cannot delete; journal durability
  failure stops before cleanup, and durable journal publication plus reclamation
  rejection remains committed journal evidence. Three host-real cases cover
  successful cleanup, stale conflict with zero deletion, and durable journal
  retention after reclamation rejection.

  Caller-directed bounded retry now wraps that transition. Only prepublication
  journal conflict retries; each typed conflict becomes the next reconciliation
  callback input, while exhausted conflict remains terminal evidence. Callback
  failure and every committed journal or reclamation outcome stop immediately;
  merge, ordering, backoff, and retention policy remain caller-owned.


  A shared policy-neutral retry directive now lets this retention/reclamation
  loop consult the caller between retryable conflicts. The hook sees the exact
  completed-attempt count after the filesystem guard is released and may return
  `Continue` or `Stop`; stopping preserves the conflict unchanged. Callers may
  synchronously wait, yield, or inspect cancellation there, while timing,
  backoff intervals, fairness, and asynchronous scheduling remain outside the
  runtime.


  Shared retry control now also centralizes exact attempt accounting for these
  synchronous loops. A private cursor starts after the first one-shot attempt,
  supplies the completed-attempt evidence seen by control hooks, and advances
  only while the caller-selected positive maximum permits it. Two focused cases
  cover exact advancement and attempt-limit exhaustion; no timing policy is
  added.


  Terminal retry evidence is centralized at the same boundary. One generic
  container owns exact attempts plus the subsystem terminal outcome, while the
  ordered-count, ordered-pair, latency, retention-journal, and guarded
  reclamation APIs keep their existing public names as aliases. One focused case
  covers borrowed and consuming access with no outcome-policy change.


  Callers may also retain an opaque typed stop reason without changing retry
  loops. A generic decision carries `Continue` or `Stop(reason)`, and a small
  stop-state adapter maps that decision to the existing runtime directive while
  preserving the exact triggering conflict and caller reason. The runtime owns
  no cancellation/yield taxonomy and never interprets that reason. Two focused
  cases cover reasonless continuation and typed stop retention.

  Cached-cycle composition now binds the opaque pair revision to typed count and
  latency owners. Versioned restore decodes both canonical members together and
  retains their exact revision. One-shot durable typed CAS preserves expected
  revision evidence and decodes bounded conflicts back into exact live owners.

  Six focused cases cover typed versioned restore, missing initialization, stale
  conflict decoding, malformed conflict rejection, committed durability failure,
  and host-real cross-instance revision advance. Blind conflict retry remains
  intentionally absent because replaying one stale whole-pair snapshot could
  overwrite concurrent telemetry; retry requires explicit fresh-state
  reconciliation or merge semantics first.

  A separate ordered telemetry-pair contract now persists the existing canonical
  `(external order + count FIFO)` document as member one and canonical latency
  as
  member two. It does not reinterpret or migrate the earlier plain count-window
  pair format. The durable watermark now exists beside latency evidence in one
  atomic publication, enabling safe future reconciliation design.

  Four focused cases cover atomic ordered-pair round trip, missing state,
  committed durability failure, and host-real cross-instance restore.
  Persistence itself still infers no order from storage contention.

  One-shot ordered-pair reconciliation now performs a fresh versioned load,
  transactionally appends one caller-ordered count batch, exactly normalizes and
  merges its latency delta, then performs one durable revision CAS. Missing
  state
  uses caller capacity and submitted latency; persisted capacity and watermark
  remain authoritative after initialization.

  Four focused cases cover initialization, exact existing-state reconciliation,
  stale-order rejection before CAS, and committed durability failure. Conflict
  remains typed non-mutating one-shot evidence.

  Caller-bounded synchronous retry now repeats the complete fresh-load
  reconciliation only after typed CAS conflict. Every attempt re-reads the
  durable watermark and latency state; a newer watermark can reject the
  submitted order rather than being silently rebased. Durable publication,
  committed sync
  failure, and every non-conflict error stop immediately. No timing, fairness,
  cancellation, or backoff policy is inferred.

  Four retry cases cover attempt-limit conflict, conflict-to-fresh-load success,
  conflict followed by exact stale-order rejection, and committed durability
  failure stopping after the first attempt.


  Ordered-pair retry now also accepts the shared synchronous retry directive.
  After a retryable conflict, the caller sees the exact completed-attempt count
  and may return `Continue` or `Stop`; stopping preserves that typed conflict.
  Continued attempts still reload and reconcile the complete pair, while the
  runtime performs no clock read, wait, backoff, or fairness action. Two focused
  cases cover one continued conflict and one caller-stopped conflict.

  The concrete filesystem adapter is likewise payload-neutral. It probes beyond
  bounded reads, stages beside one explicit destination, synchronizes staged
  bytes, and publishes by `rename` without deleting prior state first. Host-real
  cases retain missing, overwrite-or-safe-failure, oversized-read, and exact
  count/latency evidence while permitting other typed composition clients.

  A separate durability capability now confirms the destination directory only
  after replacement commits. The application reports either confirmed
  `Durable` state or committed `Published` state with exact sync failure, so
  post-publication failure is never mislabeled rollback. Two deterministic
  cases cover both outcomes and one host-real case exercises directory sync.

  Cached-cycle composition now binds both canonical count-window and latency
  histogram codecs to the durability-capable application service. Three
  adapter-neutral cases cover typed count/latency success and committed sync
  failure, while one host-real count-window case crosses the full persistence
  stack.

  A transport-neutral monotonic interval port now delimits caller-owned cached
  retry work without exposing wall-clock time. A standard `Instant` adapter owns
  exact elapsed-nanosecond conversion, and cached-cycle composition turns one
  finished interval into the existing explicit latency sample without recording
  it automatically. Two deterministic cases cover exact sampling and finish
  failure; one host-real case records a measured sample separately.

  Merge backoff/cancellation/fairness policy, async queues/timers, reclamation
  scheduling/automatic durable retention policy, general N-object
  transactions, and broader
  production orchestration remain pending.
  Persistent sequence ownership now preloads all exact mappings, reuses them
  across calls, rolls back partial loads, and releases every mapping with retry
  retention. Full chain identity is checked before buffer mutation; adapters own
  unique non-overlapping live allocations. A weighted exact-key FIFO reuses

  complete loaded chains without refreshing hits. Positive limits cover entries
  and can also cover live mappings plus admitted mapped bytes. Candidate weight
  comes from ready mapping reports; oversized candidates clean themselves, while
  fitting candidates evict repeatedly until every projected limit fits. Failed
  later insertion eviction returns failed victim/candidate cleanup ownership.

  Invalidation and full drain update usage before release. Weighted limits now
  reconfigure in place: expansion or already-fitting requests publish without
  adapter work, while shrink requests release oldest entries before publishing.
  Previous limits remain active through failure; removed keys and failed release
  ownership remain explicit for retry, and a repeated request does not release
  completed victims again. Seventeen cases cover original reuse/admission plus
  expansion, entry/mapping/byte shrink, and second-eviction failure. A separate
  immutable `Arc` lease cache now lets exact ready chains remain usable across

  threads after active lookup retirement. Retired entries keep exact weighted
  usage charged until explicit return/reconciliation observes no external
  owner. Mixed eviction releases unleased entries, retires leased entries, and
  rejects candidates blocked by resident weight. Invalidation and full drain
  use the same ownership rule; keyed cleanup failures remain retryable.
  Resident limits now expand without adapter work and shrink through active FIFO

  release/retirement. Leased victims keep exact weight charged, prior retired
  entries are not reconciled implicitly, and old limits survive blockage or
  cleanup failure. Final lease return or keyed retry permits idempotent repeated
  publication. Fourteen cases cover the original lifecycle plus entry/mapping
  shrink, live entry/byte blockage, post-return publication, and release-failure
  retry. Durable or cross-process leasing, fused objects, direct branches, and a

  concrete foreign-call shim remain open.
  Remaining fused-region emission, concrete executable-memory integration, and
  instruction-cache implementation remain pending for this TODO.
- Performance evidence pending: raw measurements plus a reproducible
  scaling/statistical summary tied to exact workload and hardware/software
  identity.
